      "summary": "Augustine opens On the Trinity by explaining why he is writing: to guard the Catholic faith against three kinds of error he sees among those who reason about God without first anchoring themselves in faith — those who picture God in bodily terms, those who project the mind's own changeable nature onto Him, and those who overreach with presumptuous speculation while claiming to transcend all creation. He asks his readers for a charitable, cooperative pact: to advance together where they agree, inquire together where they doubt, and correct one another where either errs, presenting himself throughout not as a settled authority but as a fellow seeker.\n\nAfter stating the Catholic rule of faith — that Father, Son, and Holy Spirit are one God of one substance in indivisible equality, three truly distinct Persons who nonetheless always work indivisibly — Augustine confronts the central difficulty this raises: if the three work indivisibly, how can Scripture attribute specific acts (the Father's voice at the Jordan, the Son's incarnation and death, the Spirit's descent as a dove) to only one Person at a time? He first establishes, chiefly from John's Gospel and Paul's epistles, that both the Son and the Holy Spirit are true God of one substance with the Father, not creatures, since all things were made through the Son and the exclusive worship due to God alone is commanded to be rendered to the Spirit.\n\nThe bulk of the book then develops what becomes Augustine's master interpretive key for the rest of the work: the distinction between what Scripture says of Christ \"according to the form of God,\" in which He is fully equal to the Father, and what it says \"according to the form of a servant,\" the humanity He assumed in the Incarnation, in which He is truly less than the Father. This single rule lets Augustine reconcile texts that seemed flatly contradictory to the Arian and subordinationist readers he is arguing against — \"I and my Father are one\" alongside \"My Father is greater than I,\" the Son's supposed ignorance of \"that day and hour,\" His giving up the kingdom to the Father, and His both judging and not judging the world. Augustine closes the book having shown that Scripture's seemingly demeaning statements about the Son describe the Mediator in His assumed humanity, never the eternal Word's equality with the Father."
